From: Harmony Project
Subject: kvm: invalidate guest mappings after Harmony bitmap drains

On hosted Intel nesting, automatic dirty-log reprotection can leave cached
writable L2 mappings alive. A cold inner restore followed by one L2 readback
writes five pages, but the following KVM_GET_DIRTY_LOG returns an empty bitmap.
An in-place restore then correctly selects no RAM pages and retains those
writes. Native Intel and hosted SVM controls report all five writes.

After harvesting the bitmap, invalidate the memory slot's shadow mappings
through KVM's existing slot invalidation path. The next inner entry rebuilds
its mappings with dirty logging enabled, so fresh writes are recorded even
when the bitmap was empty at the preceding drain. Keep RAM, the VM and vCPU
allocated; do not repair the workload's memory or replace incremental restore.

The extension requires the Harmony clock and automatic bitmap protection.
Manual-protection semantics stay unchanged. CONFIG_HARMONY_PVCLOCK guards the
architecture-specific include and code; ordinary Harmony kernels omit KVM,
and a KVM kernel without the Harmony clock uses the original path. VMX and
SVM share this reprotection rule. It trades extra MMU faults for correct
incremental tracking inside the controlled guest kernel.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried under the repository's
kernel-patch exception.

---
--- a/virt/kvm/kvm_main.c
+++ b/virt/kvm/kvm_main.c
@@ -52,6 +52,9 @@
 
 #include <asm/processor.h>
 #include <asm/ioctl.h>
+#ifdef CONFIG_HARMONY_PVCLOCK
+#include <asm/harmony_pvclock.h>
+#endif
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
 
 #include "coalesced_mmio.h"
@@ -2287,6 +2290,11 @@
 		}
 		KVM_MMU_UNLOCK(kvm);
 	}
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_HARMONY_PVCLOCK
+	if (!kvm->manual_dirty_log_protect && harmony_pvclock_requested())
+		kvm_arch_flush_shadow_memslot(kvm, memslot);
+#endif
 
 	if (flush)
 		kvm_flush_remote_tlbs_memslot(kvm, memslot);
